00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:17,940
What is a radical?

144
00:14:18,520 --> 00:14:23,420
It seems today that people are terrified of the term, particularly of having the

145
00:14:23,420 --> 00:14:25,240
label attached to them.

146
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:31,600
Accusing individuals or groups of being radical often serves to silence them into

147
00:14:31,600 --> 00:14:37,660
submission, thereby maintaining the existing state of affairs and, more important,

148
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:43,500
preserving the power of a select minority, who are mostly wealthy white males in

149
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:48,380
Western society. Feminism is a perfect example of this phenomenon.

150
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:54,220
The women's movement has been plagued by stereotypes, misrepresentations by the

151
00:14:54,220 --> 00:14:57,840
media, and accusations of man -hating and radicalism.

152
00:14:58,180 --> 00:15:04,060
When the basic foundation of feminism is simply that women deserve equal rights in

153
00:15:04,060 --> 00:15:05,440
all facets of life.

154
00:15:06,240 --> 00:15:12,120
When faced with a threat of being labeled radical, women back down from their worthy

155
00:15:12,120 --> 00:15:16,200
cause and consequently participate in their own oppression.

156
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:21,560
It has gotten to the point that many women are afraid to call themselves feminists

157
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:24,380
because of the stigma attached to the word.

158
00:15:24,840 --> 00:15:30,980
If people refuse to be controlled and intimidated by stigmas, the stigmas lose all

159
00:15:30,980 --> 00:15:36,500
their power. Without fear on which to feed, such stigmas can only die.

160
00:15:37,100 --> 00:15:43,300
To me, a radical is simply someone who rebels against the norm or advocates a change

161
00:15:43,300 --> 00:15:45,240
in the existing state of affairs.

162
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:51,480
On closer inspection, it becomes clear that the norm is constantly evolving and

163
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:54,220
therefore is not a constant entity.

164
00:15:54,220 --> 00:16:00,480
So why then is deviation from the present situation such a threat when the state of

165
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:05,280
affairs itself is unstable and subject to relentless transformation?

166
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:11,080
It all goes back to maintaining the power of those who have it and preventing the

167
00:16:11,080 --> 00:16:13,320
rise of those who don't.

168
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:17,440
In fact, when we look at the word radical in a historical context,

169
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:25,140
nearly every figure we now hold up as a hero was considered a radical in his or her

170
00:16:25,140 --> 00:16:28,840
time. Radicals are people who affect change.

171
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:32,300
They are the people about whom history is written.

172
00:16:33,260 --> 00:16:35,280
Abolitionists were radicals.

173
00:16:35,420 --> 00:16:38,200
Civil rights activists were radicals.

174
00:16:38,580 --> 00:16:42,640
Even the founders of our country, in their fight to win independence from England,

175
00:16:42,660 --> 00:16:48,660
were radicals. Their presence in history has changed the way our society functions,

176
00:16:48,660 --> 00:16:53,620
mainly by shifting the balance of power that previously existed.

177
00:16:54,100 --> 00:16:58,810
Of course, there are some radicals who have made a negative impact on humanity,

178
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:04,740
but undeniably there would simply be no progress without radicals.

179
00:17:04,940 --> 00:17:10,940
That being said, next time someone calls me a radical, I will accept that label with

180
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:22,800
pride. What usually happens when people are accused of being radical?

181
